+The first-person walkthrough uses the Realistic visual style with procedural natural colors and materials. Survey textures can replace the illustrated material on mapped ground surfaces and roofs.
+
+### Try the aerial texture test
+
+Use **Import survey** to open `examples/museum-texture-test.json`. In **Settings**, switch **Survey textures** off and on, then place the walker to compare the same surfaces in 3D. The sample embeds the image so it works offline. The standalone JPEG is `examples/american-history-aerial.jpg`.
+
+The sample uses [USGS NAIP aerial imagery](https://imagery.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSNAIPImagery/ImageServer) of the National Museum of American History, Washington, DC. Ground areas and the roof were traced by hand. Heights are approximate and terrain is flat.
+
+For your own geometry, open the survey first. Under **File**, choose **Attach aligned texture…** and select a north-up JPEG, PNG, or WebP cropped to the full rectangular extent of that survey. Image corners map to the survey corners. Save the scenario to package the image with the geometry. Trees, standalone objects, proposed features, and vertical walls use their existing materials. Objects and shadows captured in the source photo may be visible within textured surfaces.
 
 In Simulate, set the time of day and choose Clear, Overcast, Rain, Fog, or Snow. These scenario conditions are saved with the site. Time remains fixed during playback. The 3D preview changes sky, lighting and visibility, with falling rain or snow. Reduced-motion settings freeze precipitation. Weather and time affect route choice and optional stops. See [Pedestrian behavior](PEDESTRIANS.md) for every feature rule.
